{; EVERYTHING WRONG NOW, derived. The PULL side of exactly the facts the
 ; error path PUSHES — same facts, no second store, no acknowledgement
 ; flag, no "seen" attribute, nothing stamped when something is fixed. A
 ; problem stops being a problem when the FACTS stop saying so.
 ;
 ; The value is a MAP KEYED BY FAMILY with every empty family ABSENT, so
 ; a healthy cluster derives `{}`. That shape is deliberate twice over:
 ; it needs no `:type` discriminator on the entries (the key a family
 ; arrives under is the family), and it makes "is anything wrong?"
 ; `(seq problems)` rather than a status anybody has to maintain.

 ; One SIGNATURE, however many times it happened. Grouping is the point:
 ; a hundred errors of one signature is one problem that recurred a
 ; hundred times, and reporting it a hundred times would bury the other
 ; two. `fact` is the latest occurrence in full, so the log projection
 ; composes the ordinary per-fact line and a digger has the id, the
 ; kind, the refs and the projected data without a second lookup.
 :seon.problems/occurrences [:int {:min 1}]
 :seon.problems/error-signature [:map {:closed true} [:seon.error/signature :seon.error/signature] [:seon.error/kind :seon.error/kind] [:seon.problems/occurrences :seon.problems/occurrences] [:seon.error/fact :seon.error/fact]]

 ; A run held by a process that is NOT in the live set. Not "old" and
 ; not "expired" — no clock is consulted, because liveness is an
 ; observable fact about processes and a deadline standing in for it
 ; would be exactly the tuned constant the ruling bans.
 :seon.problems/wedged-run [:map {:closed true} [:seon.cluster.run/id :seon.cluster.run/id] [:seon.cluster.agent/id :seon.cluster.agent/id] [:seon.cluster.run/process :seon.cluster.run/process]]

 ; A run that closed carrying WHY it closed. The agent reads this in its
 ; next prompt; a human reading `problems` sees the same fact from the
 ; other side.
 :seon.problems/failed-run [:map {:closed true} [:seon.cluster.run/id :seon.cluster.run/id] [:seon.cluster.agent/id :seon.cluster.agent/id] [:seon.cluster.run/error :seon.cluster.run/error]]

 ; One form of one plan that errored. An agent's own mistake is not a
 ; core fault and never becomes an error fact — but a plan that keeps
 ; erroring is something a human wants to see, so it is a problem
 ; without being a fault, and the distinction survives into this value.
 :seon.problems/errored-receipt [:map {:closed true} [:seon.cluster.eval/id :seon.cluster.eval/id] [:seon.cluster.run/id :seon.cluster.run/id] [:seon.cluster.eval/ordinal :seon.cluster.eval/ordinal] [:seon.cluster.run.form/source :seon.cluster.run.form/source] [:seon.error/kind :seon.error/kind] [:seon.cluster.eval/error :seon.cluster.eval/error]]

 ; The request. `live-processes` is the same set `run/recover-tx` takes,
 ; by the same name, because it is the same question: which holders are
 ; real right now. Absent set = nothing is known to be live, which
 ; honestly makes every held run wedged — the caller that cannot answer
 ; must not get a quieter answer than the truth.
 ; `live-processes` references the ONE global registration (context.edn)
 ; — the same set, by the same name, as the capture and every request
 ; builder carry (§9 of the context-blocks contract package).
 :seon.problems/request [:map {:closed true} [:seon.cluster.run/live-processes :seon.cluster.run/live-processes]]

 :seon.problems/problems [:map {:closed true} [:seon.problems/error-signatures {:optional true} [:vector :seon.problems/error-signature]] [:seon.problems/wedged-runs {:optional true} [:vector :seon.problems/wedged-run]] [:seon.problems/failed-runs {:optional true} [:vector :seon.problems/failed-run]] [:seon.problems/errored-receipts {:optional true} [:vector :seon.problems/errored-receipt]] [:seon.render/ai {:optional true} :seon.render/projection] [:seon.render/log {:optional true} :seon.render/projection] [:seon.render/html {:optional true} :seon.render/html]]}
